12. Complete End-to-End Business Flow

Scenario: A student enrolls in a course, makes payment, receives an assignment and gets a result.

Step 1: student-service registers the student.
Step 2: course-service and instructor-service add the course and instructor.
Step 3: enrollment-service verifies student and course through OpenFeign + Eureka and creates the enrollment.
Step 4: payment-service verifies student and enrollment and records the payment.
Step 5: assignment-service verifies course and instructor and creates the assignment.
Step 6: result-service verifies student, assignment and enrollment and saves the result.

This scenario should be used as the main project demonstration.

13. Eureka Server and Registration Requirements

Create eureka-server as a separate Spring Boot application and configure it as the Eureka Server. All seven business services must act as Eureka Clients.

Eureka Registry
   |
   +-- student-service
   +-- course-service
   +-- instructor-service
   +-- enrollment-service
   +-- payment-service
   +-- assignment-service
   +-- result-service

The Eureka Dashboard must show all seven business services as registered/available.

14. OpenFeign Requirements

For every mandatory relationship in the communication matrix, create an appropriate Feign client in the calling service.

The Feign client must target the required service by its service name, while Eureka discovers an available instance.

Calling Service
      |
      v
  OpenFeign
      |
      v
Target Service
      |
      v
Available Service Instance
(discovered through Eureka)

Do not hard-code another service's IP address or port as the discovery mechanism.

15. Database Requirements

Each business microservice must own its business data. Separate databases or clearly separated schemas/tables may be used according to the implementation.

- student-service owns student data.
- course-service owns course data.
- instructor-service owns instructor data.
- enrollment-service owns enrollment data.
- payment-service owns payment data.
- assignment-service owns assignment data.
- result-service owns result data.
- A service must not directly access another service's database.

16. REST API Expectations

Each service must expose meaningful REST endpoints for its own CRUD operations. Exact URL naming may be designed by the student.

POST   /students
GET    /students/{id}
GET    /students
PUT    /students/{id}
DELETE /students/{id}

Use the same CRUD concept for courses, instructors, enrollments, payments, assignments and results.

17. Validation and Business Rules

- Reject enrollment if student does not exist.
- Reject enrollment if course does not exist.
- For payment, verify referenced student and enrollment.
- For assignment, verify course and instructor.
- For result, verify student, assignment and enrollment.
- Return suitable HTTP status codes and meaningful error responses.
- Do not create records for invalid references.

18. Service Failure Consideration

Microservices are independent applications. If a required target service is unavailable, the calling service should return an understandable error rather than pretending that remote data was obtained.

Optional enhancement: implement fallback/error-handling mechanisms if covered in the course.

